1
00:00:00,096 --> 00:00:02,011
On day 91, you are still driving.

2
00:00:02,011 --> 00:00:03,704
The headlights are off.

3
00:00:04,190 --> 00:00:05,953
The woman steers by the shapes of

4
00:00:05,953 --> 00:00:07,509
the rooftops against the sky,

5
00:00:07,509 --> 00:00:09,877
and every few streets she gives the horn

6
00:00:09,877 --> 00:00:11,994
one short blast, the way you would

7
00:00:11,994 --> 00:00:14,308
remind a dog that you are still there.

8
00:00:14,308 --> 00:00:15,687
In the side mirror,

9
00:00:15,687 --> 00:00:17,430
the street behind you is moving.

10
00:00:17,430 --> 00:00:19,524
It is the tail of the river,

11
00:00:19,524 --> 00:00:21,372
and it is following you.

12
00:00:21,372 --> 00:00:22,851
Your name is Patch.

13
00:00:22,851 --> 00:00:23,772
Ninety days ago,

14
00:00:23,772 --> 00:00:25,749
you were waiting for a bus.

15
00:00:25,749 --> 00:00:28,569
Since then, you have lived under a bus

16
00:00:28,569 --> 00:00:30,383
depot with Nora, who used to clean it,

17
00:00:30,383 --> 00:00:32,643
and for the last month with Wes,

18
00:00:32,643 --> 00:00:35,377
who whispers all day and snores even quieter.

19
00:00:35,377 --> 00:00:37,642
Yesterday, the three of you filled the

20
00:00:37,642 --> 00:00:39,842
depot yard with thousands of the dead.

21
00:00:39,842 --> 00:00:42,437
Then you stepped into the street and shouted,

22
00:00:42,437 --> 00:00:44,453
so Nora could close the gate.

23
00:00:44,453 --> 00:00:45,461
She closed it.

24
00:00:45,461 --> 00:00:47,349
You were on the wrong side.

25
00:00:47,349 --> 00:00:49,406
Now you are in a truck with a

26
00:00:49,406 --> 00:00:51,124
woman whose name you don't know,

27
00:00:51,124 --> 00:00:52,934
whose face you haven't seen,

28
00:00:52,934 --> 00:00:55,498
and who hasn't said a word for an hour.

29
00:00:55,498 --> 00:00:57,361
"Where are we going?"

30
00:00:57,361 --> 00:00:58,301
you whisper.

31
00:00:58,301 --> 00:00:59,612
"Up," she says.

32
00:01:00,369 --> 00:01:02,770
Before dawn, the road starts to climb.

33
00:01:02,770 --> 00:01:04,231
It is the bypass,

34
00:01:04,231 --> 00:01:06,525
a long concrete ramp that lifts over the

35
00:01:06,525 --> 00:01:09,006
railway and comes down on the far side.

36
00:01:09,006 --> 00:01:11,555
At the very top, she stops in the

37
00:01:11,555 --> 00:01:13,552
middle of the road and leans on the horn.

38
00:01:13,552 --> 00:01:15,111
Not a short blast this time.

39
00:01:15,111 --> 00:01:15,880
A long one.

40
00:01:15,880 --> 00:01:18,573
It rolls out over the whole town.

41
00:01:18,573 --> 00:01:19,983
Then she turns the key,

42
00:01:19,983 --> 00:01:21,270
and the engine dies.

43
00:01:21,270 --> 00:01:23,245
The truck starts to roll.

44
00:01:23,245 --> 00:01:24,914
Down the far side of the ramp,

45
00:01:24,914 --> 00:01:27,255
in total silence, with no lights,

46
00:01:27,255 --> 00:01:31,049
faster and faster, the tyres hissing on the concrete.

47
00:01:31,049 --> 00:01:32,579
You grip the dashboard.

48
00:01:32,579 --> 00:01:35,043
She holds the wheel with two fingers,

49
00:01:35,043 --> 00:01:36,098
like a teacup.

50
00:01:36,098 --> 00:01:38,173
"They walk to the last

51
00:01:38,173 --> 00:01:40,300
sound they heard," she says.

52
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:42,048
"So I give them a last

53
00:01:42,048 --> 00:01:44,181
sound somewhere I'm not."

54
00:01:44,748 --> 00:01:45,480
At the bottom,

55
00:01:45,480 --> 00:01:47,670
the truck coasts along an empty road,

56
00:01:47,670 --> 00:01:50,893
slows, and stops beside a dark petrol station.

57
00:01:51,412 --> 00:01:52,672
She doesn't turn the

58
00:01:52,672 --> 00:01:54,051
key again for two hours.

59
00:01:54,051 --> 00:01:56,546
By then, a kilometer is a wall.

60
00:01:56,546 --> 00:01:58,036
When the sun comes up,

61
00:01:58,036 --> 00:02:00,557
you can see the flyover behind you,

62
00:02:00,557 --> 00:02:02,474
black from one railing to the other.

63
00:02:02,474 --> 00:02:05,155
The tail of the river is standing at the top,

64
00:02:05,155 --> 00:02:06,064
facing nowhere,

65
00:02:06,064 --> 00:02:09,537
waiting for a horn that is never going to blast again.

66
00:02:09,537 --> 00:02:12,308
Near the front, in the middle of the road,

67
00:02:12,308 --> 00:02:13,900
there is an olive vest.

68
00:02:14,226 --> 00:02:17,049
The Doorman has a new door.

69
00:02:17,049 --> 00:02:19,510
Days 92 and 93 are spent in

70
00:02:19,510 --> 00:02:22,200
slow loops through the lower town.

71
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:24,270
The woman stops often,

72
00:02:24,270 --> 00:02:27,452
checks her mirrors, and only moves on when nothing is

73
00:02:27,452 --> 00:02:29,650
following that she didn't mean to bring.

74
00:02:30,281 --> 00:02:32,331
You sleep in the cab, sitting up.

75
00:02:32,331 --> 00:02:34,453
It is your first night in three months

76
00:02:34,453 --> 00:02:35,954
outside the depot walls,

77
00:02:35,954 --> 00:02:40,064
and you keep waking up to check that the sky hasn't moved.

78
00:02:40,064 --> 00:02:41,537
At sunset on day 92,

79
00:02:41,537 --> 00:02:43,510
she hands you the radio.

80
00:02:44,014 --> 00:02:45,579
"Depot," you whisper.

81
00:02:45,579 --> 00:02:47,366
"This is the truck."

82
00:02:47,773 --> 00:02:48,997
A long hiss.

83
00:02:48,997 --> 00:02:50,032
Then Nora.

84
00:02:50,032 --> 00:02:51,462
"Still here."

85
00:02:52,206 --> 00:02:53,787
She tells you everything

86
00:02:53,787 --> 00:02:55,621
in one breath, very quietly.

87
00:02:55,621 --> 00:02:56,564
The gate held.

88
00:02:56,564 --> 00:02:59,302
The yard is full right up to the canopy.

89
00:02:59,741 --> 00:03:01,394
Nobody can step onto the gantry

90
00:03:01,394 --> 00:03:03,426
without a thousand faces turning up.

91
00:03:03,426 --> 00:03:05,214
So they live in the bunker now,

92
00:03:05,214 --> 00:03:07,904
and only go upstairs to flash the light.

93
00:03:07,904 --> 00:03:10,900
"Your ceiling had hundreds," says Wes,

94
00:03:10,900 --> 00:03:12,642
leaning into the radio.

95
00:03:12,642 --> 00:03:14,527
"Ours has thousands.

96
00:03:14,527 --> 00:03:16,772
We're thinking of charging rent."

97
00:03:16,772 --> 00:03:18,823
You ask Nora if she's alright.

98
00:03:18,823 --> 00:03:21,457
There is a pause long enough to say no in.

99
00:03:21,831 --> 00:03:23,123
"You shouted," she says.

100
00:03:23,123 --> 00:03:24,266
"You closed it."

101
00:03:24,865 --> 00:03:25,635
"I know.

102
00:03:25,635 --> 00:03:28,043
I'm angry at both of us."

103
00:03:28,043 --> 00:03:30,210
On day 93, the radio says the

104
00:03:30,210 --> 00:03:32,923
same thing at sunset, in fewer words.

105
00:03:32,923 --> 00:03:33,708
Still here.

106
00:03:33,708 --> 00:03:36,755
You realize it is the only news you need.

107
00:03:37,226 --> 00:03:40,122
On day 94, the truck finally turns uphill.

108
00:03:40,122 --> 00:03:42,497
The old waterworks sits on the

109
00:03:42,497 --> 00:03:43,485
very top of the hill,

110
00:03:43,485 --> 00:03:45,109
behind a tall wire fence,

111
00:03:45,109 --> 00:03:48,406
with three round water tanks and a brick pump house.

112
00:03:48,406 --> 00:03:50,517
Two people swing the fence gate open,

113
00:03:50,517 --> 00:03:52,100
and it doesn't make a sound.

114
00:03:52,100 --> 00:03:53,679
You have a good idea

115
00:03:53,679 --> 00:03:54,855
who supplies their oil.

116
00:03:54,855 --> 00:03:56,179
The woman parks and climbs

117
00:03:56,179 --> 00:03:57,203
down from the cab,

118
00:03:57,203 --> 00:04:00,063
and for the first time, you see her in daylight.

119
00:04:00,436 --> 00:04:02,123
A pair of brown driving goggles

120
00:04:02,123 --> 00:04:04,564
pushed up on top of her round head.

121
00:04:04,564 --> 00:04:05,640
A long grey scarf

122
00:04:05,640 --> 00:04:07,434
wrapped once around her neck.

123
00:04:07,434 --> 00:04:09,532
She looks like someone who has

124
00:04:09,532 --> 00:04:11,102
spent three months telling strangers

125
00:04:11,102 --> 00:04:13,345
what to do, and being right about it.

126
00:04:13,345 --> 00:04:15,767
"You expected horns," she says.

127
00:04:16,124 --> 00:04:17,415
"I expected a face."

128
00:04:17,415 --> 00:04:17,823
"Well.

129
00:04:17,823 --> 00:04:19,182
Now you've seen it.

130
00:04:19,182 --> 00:04:21,178
Don't spread it around."

131
00:04:21,178 --> 00:04:22,345
About a dozen people

132
00:04:22,345 --> 00:04:24,313
come out to look at you.

133
00:04:24,313 --> 00:04:26,487
They look at your orange bandage.

134
00:04:26,487 --> 00:04:27,926
Every one of them has

135
00:04:27,926 --> 00:04:29,303
a blue one somewhere.

136
00:04:29,303 --> 00:04:31,539
Someone hands you a cup of water.

137
00:04:31,539 --> 00:04:35,342
It is cold and clean and tastes of absolutely nothing,

138
00:04:35,342 --> 00:04:36,367
which is the best

139
00:04:36,367 --> 00:04:38,981
thing you have tasted since day one.

140
00:04:38,981 --> 00:04:41,350
In the old filter beds behind the tanks,

141
00:04:41,350 --> 00:04:42,805
they grow potatoes.

142
00:04:43,163 --> 00:04:44,234
Along the fence,

143
00:04:44,234 --> 00:04:46,567
three apple trees lean over the wire as

144
00:04:46,567 --> 00:04:48,358
if they would like to leave.

145
00:04:48,358 --> 00:04:49,285
Wes was right.

146
00:04:49,285 --> 00:04:51,624
It is a very good view.

147
00:04:51,624 --> 00:04:53,473
Days 95 to 97 are for

148
00:04:53,473 --> 00:04:55,587
learning to sleep above ground.

149
00:04:55,587 --> 00:04:57,087
The hill gives you a room

150
00:04:57,087 --> 00:04:58,711
in the pump house,

151
00:04:58,711 --> 00:05:01,300
with a mattress, a blanket and a window.

152
00:05:01,300 --> 00:05:03,202
On day one, you decided that not

153
00:05:03,202 --> 00:05:06,096
having a view was excellent interior design.

154
00:05:06,455 --> 00:05:08,683
Now you lie awake and stare at yours.

155
00:05:08,683 --> 00:05:10,273
The silence is wrong.

156
00:05:10,728 --> 00:05:12,456
There are no feet on the ceiling.

157
00:05:12,456 --> 00:05:14,128
There is barely a ceiling.

158
00:05:14,583 --> 00:05:15,751
Every time the wind

159
00:05:15,751 --> 00:05:17,562
rattles the fence, you sit up.

160
00:05:17,562 --> 00:05:19,427
So at midnight on day 95,

161
00:05:19,427 --> 00:05:21,625
you borrow a flashlight and climb the ladder

162
00:05:21,625 --> 00:05:24,221
on the side of the tallest water tank.

163
00:05:24,221 --> 00:05:25,004
From the top,

164
00:05:25,004 --> 00:05:27,206
the whole town lies dark below you.

165
00:05:27,206 --> 00:05:28,147
The motorway.

166
00:05:28,147 --> 00:05:30,224
The split in the road.

167
00:05:30,224 --> 00:05:32,167
The flyover, with its black line.

168
00:05:32,167 --> 00:05:33,521
And far down the hill,

169
00:05:33,521 --> 00:05:35,975
a dark square with a canopy.

170
00:05:35,975 --> 00:05:36,747
The depot.

171
00:05:36,747 --> 00:05:37,867
On the hour,

172
00:05:37,867 --> 00:05:40,872
at its far corner, a small light blinks once.

173
00:05:40,872 --> 00:05:41,956
Still here.

174
00:05:42,346 --> 00:05:43,857
You point the flashlight at it,

175
00:05:43,857 --> 00:05:45,230
and press the switch.

176
00:05:45,733 --> 00:05:46,193
Once.

177
00:05:46,193 --> 00:05:48,567
On the radio the next evening,

178
00:05:48,567 --> 00:05:50,344
Wes is delighted.

179
00:05:50,344 --> 00:05:51,152
"We saw you.

180
00:05:51,152 --> 00:05:53,945
You blink like a nervous lighthouse."

181
00:05:54,544 --> 00:05:56,522
Nora doesn't mention it at all.

182
00:05:56,522 --> 00:05:58,411
But at the next hour,

183
00:05:58,411 --> 00:06:00,188
and every hour after that,

184
00:06:00,188 --> 00:06:02,752
the light at the depot blinks twice.

185
00:06:02,752 --> 00:06:04,207
Once for still here.

186
00:06:04,207 --> 00:06:05,244
Once for you.

187
00:06:05,244 --> 00:06:08,667
On day 98, you start earning your water.

188
00:06:08,667 --> 00:06:10,638
At dawn, the truck goes out to herd,

189
00:06:10,638 --> 00:06:12,218
and you go with it.

190
00:06:12,218 --> 00:06:13,590
You learn the code you first

191
00:06:13,590 --> 00:06:14,820
heard about on the radio,

192
00:06:14,820 --> 00:06:16,559
back when Wes was only a voice.

193
00:06:16,559 --> 00:06:18,981
One short blast at every corner.

194
00:06:18,981 --> 00:06:21,401
Slow, so the slow ones can keep up.

195
00:06:21,401 --> 00:06:23,945
Never stop where you want to come back.

196
00:06:23,945 --> 00:06:24,709
All morning,

197
00:06:24,709 --> 00:06:28,513
you pull small crowds away from the bottom of the hill.

198
00:06:28,513 --> 00:06:29,165
Twenty here.

199
00:06:29,165 --> 00:06:30,325
Forty there.

200
00:06:30,325 --> 00:06:32,649
They come out of shops and doorways

201
00:06:32,649 --> 00:06:34,865
and follow the engine in long,

202
00:06:34,865 --> 00:06:37,706
ragged lines, like the worst parade in history.

203
00:06:38,160 --> 00:06:40,062
Then the truck turns a corner,

204
00:06:40,062 --> 00:06:42,738
coasts, and leaves them somewhere useless.

205
00:06:42,738 --> 00:06:46,201
By day 100, you understand the hill.

206
00:06:46,201 --> 00:06:47,272
Clean water.

207
00:06:47,272 --> 00:06:48,096
Potatoes.

208
00:06:48,096 --> 00:06:49,055
A view.

209
00:06:49,477 --> 00:06:52,072
And every single morning, this.

210
00:06:52,751 --> 00:06:53,745
It never ends.

211
00:06:53,745 --> 00:06:56,780
Every crowd you lead away drifts back,

212
00:06:56,780 --> 00:06:59,515
the way rainwater finds the lowest street.

213
00:06:59,515 --> 00:07:01,181
On the third morning,

214
00:07:01,181 --> 00:07:03,668
the truck passes the bottom of your old street.

215
00:07:03,668 --> 00:07:05,567
You see the depot wall.

216
00:07:05,567 --> 00:07:06,981
The top of the canopy.

217
00:07:06,981 --> 00:07:09,999
The sign, still hanging over the road.

218
00:07:09,999 --> 00:07:10,857
THREE ALIVE.

219
00:07:10,857 --> 00:07:13,615
And at the far end of the gantry,

220
00:07:13,615 --> 00:07:16,481
a bright spark, flashing in the sun.

221
00:07:16,481 --> 00:07:17,447
Wes's mirror.

222
00:07:17,447 --> 00:07:19,102
He has been waiting all

223
00:07:19,102 --> 00:07:21,281
morning for the sound of the truck.

224
00:07:21,281 --> 00:07:22,672
You don't have a mirror.

225
00:07:22,672 --> 00:07:26,030
So you put your hand out of the window and wave,

226
00:07:26,030 --> 00:07:28,585
very slowly, like a man who

227
00:07:28,585 --> 00:07:31,383
has never been interesting in his life.

228
00:07:31,383 --> 00:07:32,607
On day 101,

229
00:07:32,607 --> 00:07:34,516
you learn what the hill calls your home.

230
00:07:34,516 --> 00:07:35,990
They call it the pen.

231
00:07:35,990 --> 00:07:36,805
Not unkindly.

232
00:07:36,805 --> 00:07:38,145
The way you would name

233
00:07:38,145 --> 00:07:40,251
something you are glad exists.

234
00:07:40,624 --> 00:07:42,820
The pen keeps the river off the hill.

235
00:07:42,820 --> 00:07:45,612
The pen is why they can sleep at night.

236
00:07:45,612 --> 00:07:46,563
And Nora and Wes,

237
00:07:46,563 --> 00:07:48,429
you learn, are the keepers.

238
00:07:48,786 --> 00:07:50,176
You ask the woman when

239
00:07:50,176 --> 00:07:51,626
she can drive you back.

240
00:07:51,626 --> 00:07:53,077
"I can't," she says.

241
00:07:53,077 --> 00:07:55,132
She takes you to the window of the

242
00:07:55,132 --> 00:07:57,943
pump house and points down at the depot.

243
00:07:57,943 --> 00:07:59,574
"That yard is full.

244
00:07:59,932 --> 00:08:02,613
Every one of them is leaning on that gate.

245
00:08:02,613 --> 00:08:04,987
If I park an engine outside it,

246
00:08:04,987 --> 00:08:07,031
they all lean the same way at once.

247
00:08:07,031 --> 00:08:09,418
You've seen what that does."

248
00:08:09,418 --> 00:08:10,724
You have.

249
00:08:11,403 --> 00:08:13,305
On day 78, the bolt jumped out of its

250
00:08:13,305 --> 00:08:15,760
hole with a crowd a fraction of this size.

251
00:08:15,760 --> 00:08:17,329
"An engine at that gate

252
00:08:17,329 --> 00:08:19,273
isn't a ride," she says.

253
00:08:19,273 --> 00:08:20,131
"It's a key."

254
00:08:20,131 --> 00:08:21,418
She makes you the

255
00:08:21,418 --> 00:08:24,045
offer she made on day 64.

256
00:08:24,045 --> 00:08:24,639
A bed.

257
00:08:24,639 --> 00:08:26,043
Clean water.

258
00:08:26,400 --> 00:08:27,406
Daylight.

259
00:08:27,406 --> 00:08:28,815
You can stay.

260
00:08:28,815 --> 00:08:30,856
"I could have come up

261
00:08:30,856 --> 00:08:32,270
here on day 70," you say.

262
00:08:32,270 --> 00:08:34,266
"I stayed down there for her."

263
00:08:34,266 --> 00:08:35,866
"I know," she says.

264
00:08:35,866 --> 00:08:37,652
"That's exactly why I

265
00:08:37,652 --> 00:08:39,077
don't give out my name."

266
00:08:39,077 --> 00:08:40,624
She doesn't explain.

267
00:08:40,997 --> 00:08:41,850
By day 103,

268
00:08:41,850 --> 00:08:44,566
you decide you don't need her to.

269
00:08:44,987 --> 00:08:46,084
You need a plan.

270
00:08:46,084 --> 00:08:47,610
And you need rain.

271
00:08:48,368 --> 00:08:50,782
On day 104, the radio is late.

272
00:08:50,782 --> 00:08:52,731
When Nora finally answers,

273
00:08:52,731 --> 00:08:55,776
she is whispering even more quietly than usual.

274
00:08:56,311 --> 00:08:58,144
In the night, the yard shifted.

275
00:08:58,144 --> 00:08:59,746
Thousands of them,

276
00:08:59,746 --> 00:09:03,290
packed against each other, leaned at once toward the loading bay.

277
00:09:03,696 --> 00:09:05,825
The bay door boomed like a drum.

278
00:09:05,825 --> 00:09:08,067
The wall of suitcases slid.

279
00:09:08,067 --> 00:09:09,625
One of them tumbled down the

280
00:09:09,625 --> 00:09:11,226
corridor and burst open,

281
00:09:11,226 --> 00:09:13,668
full of somebody's summer shirts.

282
00:09:13,668 --> 00:09:14,674
"And the door?"

283
00:09:14,674 --> 00:09:15,620
you ask.

284
00:09:15,979 --> 00:09:18,023
"The latch held," she says.

285
00:09:18,023 --> 00:09:19,255
Behind her,

286
00:09:19,255 --> 00:09:22,285
Wes whispers, "Tell him whose latch."

287
00:09:22,949 --> 00:09:24,621
"Wes's latch held," says Nora,

288
00:09:24,621 --> 00:09:26,562
in the voice of someone handing

289
00:09:26,562 --> 00:09:28,538
out a medal she doesn't approve of.

290
00:09:28,538 --> 00:09:31,127
Management came through after all.

291
00:09:31,127 --> 00:09:33,022
But the push doesn't stop.

292
00:09:33,022 --> 00:09:34,528
All through day 105,

293
00:09:34,528 --> 00:09:37,687
the bay door creaks in a slow rhythm, like breathing.

294
00:09:37,997 --> 00:09:40,070
Nora and Wes carry every suitcase

295
00:09:40,070 --> 00:09:41,107
back into place,

296
00:09:41,107 --> 00:09:43,607
then add the empty shelves from the store,

297
00:09:43,607 --> 00:09:45,637
one at a time, without letting them touch.

298
00:09:46,236 --> 00:09:47,096
On day 106,

299
00:09:47,096 --> 00:09:50,262
Wes counts what is left in the bunker.

300
00:09:50,588 --> 00:09:52,238
Three of the four water jugs.

301
00:09:52,238 --> 00:09:54,362
Food for maybe six weeks,

302
00:09:54,362 --> 00:09:57,048
if nobody eats the sugared almonds out of boredom.

303
00:09:57,048 --> 00:09:58,243
Nobody promises.

304
00:09:58,618 --> 00:10:00,654
And from the top of the water tank,

305
00:10:00,654 --> 00:10:03,480
at midnight, you watch the depot blink twice,

306
00:10:03,480 --> 00:10:04,278
then wait,

307
00:10:04,278 --> 00:10:09,281
then blink twice again, like a light that is out of breath.

308
00:10:09,281 --> 00:10:11,783
On day 107, you draw the route on the

309
00:10:11,783 --> 00:10:13,855
back of an old waterworks form,

310
00:10:13,855 --> 00:10:16,422
the way Nora drew the corridor on day two:

311
00:10:16,422 --> 00:10:19,602
thick lines wherever something can go wrong.

312
00:10:19,602 --> 00:10:21,551
Down the hill road, on foot.

313
00:10:21,893 --> 00:10:23,183
Through the split.

314
00:10:23,183 --> 00:10:24,678
Down the long street,

315
00:10:24,678 --> 00:10:28,132
past the side road where the truck picked you up.

316
00:10:28,132 --> 00:10:31,107
To the depot wall, right under the sign.

317
00:10:31,107 --> 00:10:32,199
No engine.

318
00:10:32,199 --> 00:10:33,093
No horn.

319
00:10:33,093 --> 00:10:34,648
Just you,

320
00:10:34,648 --> 00:10:37,483
being uninteresting, for two kilometers.

321
00:10:38,018 --> 00:10:38,577
Then up.

322
00:10:38,577 --> 00:10:40,440
Nora will lower the rope from

323
00:10:40,440 --> 00:10:42,587
the far end of the gantry,

324
00:10:42,587 --> 00:10:45,508
the same rope you hauled tins up on day 70.

325
00:10:45,866 --> 00:10:47,241
She doesn't like it.

326
00:10:47,241 --> 00:10:50,355
She agrees anyway, then goes quiet on the radio,

327
00:10:50,355 --> 00:10:52,666
which is how you know she means it.

328
00:10:53,024 --> 00:10:54,802
But you need one more thing.

329
00:10:54,802 --> 00:10:56,021
You need rain.

330
00:10:56,780 --> 00:10:58,202
Rain covers footsteps.

331
00:10:58,202 --> 00:11:00,151
And rain pulls every walker in

332
00:11:00,151 --> 00:11:01,709
the yard in under the canopy,

333
00:11:01,709 --> 00:11:03,723
away from the gate and the wall,

334
00:11:04,097 --> 00:11:06,072
the way it has since the first week.

335
00:11:06,072 --> 00:11:07,705
In the rain, the yard will be

336
00:11:07,705 --> 00:11:09,566
watching the coach, not the sky.

337
00:11:09,566 --> 00:11:10,750
The woman reads your

338
00:11:10,750 --> 00:11:12,537
drawing for a long time.

339
00:11:12,537 --> 00:11:14,708
"This is stupid," she says.

340
00:11:15,082 --> 00:11:17,085
Then she marks the three streets where

341
00:11:17,085 --> 00:11:19,087
her herding left crowds this month,

342
00:11:19,087 --> 00:11:22,505
and crosses out your route through two of them.

343
00:11:22,505 --> 00:11:23,982
On day 110,

344
00:11:23,982 --> 00:11:26,229
she puts a radio in your hand.

345
00:11:26,229 --> 00:11:29,289
"The hill only has three," she says.

346
00:11:29,289 --> 00:11:31,385
"Don't make it two."

347
00:11:31,385 --> 00:11:33,923
Day 111 is dry.

348
00:11:33,923 --> 00:11:36,441
So is day 112.

349
00:11:36,879 --> 00:11:38,206
You check the sky three

350
00:11:38,206 --> 00:11:41,037
times a day, a habit you picked up

351
00:11:41,037 --> 00:11:43,119
on day 50 and never put down.

352
00:11:43,119 --> 00:11:45,153
You pack the orange bag.

353
00:11:45,543 --> 00:11:46,164
Water.

354
00:11:46,164 --> 00:11:47,050
Crackers.

355
00:11:47,050 --> 00:11:49,035
The blue bandages.

356
00:11:49,634 --> 00:11:50,654
The hill's radio,

357
00:11:50,654 --> 00:11:54,287
wrapped in a shirt so it can't knock against anything.

358
00:11:54,287 --> 00:11:57,358
And six apples from the trees by the fence,

359
00:11:57,358 --> 00:11:59,259
the first fresh food you have

360
00:11:59,259 --> 00:12:02,665
touched since you were waiting for a bus.

361
00:12:02,665 --> 00:12:05,205
On the evening of day 113,

362
00:12:05,205 --> 00:12:07,400
the woman sits beside you on top of the

363
00:12:07,400 --> 00:12:09,867
water tank and watches the depot blink.

364
00:12:09,867 --> 00:12:11,773
"Wes once asked you on the

365
00:12:11,773 --> 00:12:12,915
radio," she says,

366
00:12:12,915 --> 00:12:15,605
"whether Patch was a name or an injury."

367
00:12:16,461 --> 00:12:16,897
"Both."

368
00:12:16,897 --> 00:12:19,078
"Mine's only a name," she says.

369
00:12:19,078 --> 00:12:21,237
"Nothing happened to it.

370
00:12:21,237 --> 00:12:23,047
I'd like to keep it that way."

371
00:12:23,047 --> 00:12:25,802
"Wes says names are for people who stay."

372
00:12:25,802 --> 00:12:27,679
"Wes talks a lot."

373
00:12:28,246 --> 00:12:29,878
"I'm staying too," you say.

374
00:12:29,878 --> 00:12:31,308
"Just not up here."

375
00:12:31,939 --> 00:12:33,363
She looks at you for a long

376
00:12:33,363 --> 00:12:35,290
time over the top of her scarf.

377
00:12:35,712 --> 00:12:36,332
For a moment,

378
00:12:36,332 --> 00:12:38,228
you think she is going to tell you.

379
00:12:38,779 --> 00:12:40,355
Then the depot blinks twice,

380
00:12:40,355 --> 00:12:42,339
and she looks at that instead.

381
00:12:42,793 --> 00:12:46,882
On day 114, the clouds come in low over

382
00:12:46,882 --> 00:12:49,816
the hill and sit there, heavy and grey.

383
00:12:50,317 --> 00:12:51,786
Clouds are not rain.

384
00:12:51,786 --> 00:12:55,326
You have known that since day 39.

385
00:12:55,326 --> 00:12:58,556
Day 115 starts with the sound

386
00:12:58,556 --> 00:13:00,533
of rain on the pump house roof,

387
00:13:00,533 --> 00:13:02,407
and you are already dressed.

388
00:13:02,407 --> 00:13:05,403
The fence gate opens without a whisper.

389
00:13:05,953 --> 00:13:07,923
The woman stands beside it with

390
00:13:07,923 --> 00:13:09,893
her scarf pulled over her head.

391
00:13:09,893 --> 00:13:12,383
She doesn't wish you luck.

392
00:13:12,383 --> 00:13:13,142
She says,

393
00:13:13,142 --> 00:13:15,824
"One short blast at every corner."

394
00:13:15,824 --> 00:13:17,661
You don't have a horn.

395
00:13:17,661 --> 00:13:19,836
You suspect she knows.

396
00:13:19,836 --> 00:13:21,858
The hill road runs with water.

397
00:13:21,858 --> 00:13:24,117
You walk down the middle of it,

398
00:13:24,117 --> 00:13:27,078
slowly, steadily, without stopping.

399
00:13:27,692 --> 00:13:28,469
At the split,

400
00:13:28,469 --> 00:13:32,493
the motorway ramp climbs away into the rain toward the city.

401
00:13:32,493 --> 00:13:34,453
You take the other road.

402
00:13:34,453 --> 00:13:34,923
Down.

403
00:13:34,923 --> 00:13:37,072
The lower town is grey

404
00:13:37,072 --> 00:13:38,801
and full of small noises.

405
00:13:38,801 --> 00:13:39,701
Drips.

406
00:13:39,701 --> 00:13:40,305
Gutters.

407
00:13:40,305 --> 00:13:42,956
A shop sign creaking on its chain.

408
00:13:43,636 --> 00:13:44,823
Rain makes everything a

409
00:13:44,823 --> 00:13:46,009
little bit interesting,

410
00:13:46,009 --> 00:13:48,366
which means nothing is very interesting.

411
00:13:48,366 --> 00:13:50,280
That is the whole plan.

412
00:13:51,265 --> 00:13:53,752
At a bus stop, a walker sits on the bench,

413
00:13:53,752 --> 00:13:55,722
facing the road, waiting.

414
00:13:55,722 --> 00:13:57,611
You pass within arm's reach.

415
00:13:57,611 --> 00:13:59,018
It doesn't turn.

416
00:13:59,457 --> 00:14:01,513
On day one, that bench was yours.

417
00:14:01,513 --> 00:14:02,734
Past the side road

418
00:14:02,734 --> 00:14:04,741
where the truck came for you.

419
00:14:04,741 --> 00:14:06,198
Into your old street.

420
00:14:06,198 --> 00:14:08,450
And there is the gate.

421
00:14:08,450 --> 00:14:10,127
Solid, wet, closed.

422
00:14:10,127 --> 00:14:12,069
You walk right past it.

423
00:14:12,069 --> 00:14:14,467
From the other side comes a sound

424
00:14:14,467 --> 00:14:15,487
like the sea:

425
00:14:15,487 --> 00:14:19,118
thousands of feet shuffling on concrete in the rain,

426
00:14:19,118 --> 00:14:21,509
all of them heading for the canopy.

427
00:14:21,509 --> 00:14:24,237
The gate creaks once as you pass.

428
00:14:24,237 --> 00:14:25,281
You don't stop.

429
00:14:25,281 --> 00:14:26,820
You don't breathe.

430
00:14:27,323 --> 00:14:29,004
The sign hangs over the street at

431
00:14:29,004 --> 00:14:31,282
the end of the gantry, heavy with rain.

432
00:14:32,363 --> 00:14:33,019
THREE ALIVE.

433
00:14:33,019 --> 00:14:36,018
The yellow paint has started to run.

434
00:14:36,376 --> 00:14:39,283
And beside it, a rope comes down the wall,

435
00:14:39,283 --> 00:14:42,158
swaying, with a loop tied at the bottom.

436
00:14:42,158 --> 00:14:43,938
On the gantry above,

437
00:14:43,938 --> 00:14:46,168
a small figure with a purple band on

438
00:14:46,168 --> 00:14:48,582
her arm holds the other end.

439
00:14:48,582 --> 00:14:50,734
Behind her, a red hat.

440
00:14:50,734 --> 00:14:53,264
You put your foot in the loop,

441
00:14:53,264 --> 00:14:54,100
and you climb.

442
00:14:54,100 --> 00:14:56,908
The rope is wet, and so are your hands,

443
00:14:56,908 --> 00:14:59,364
and the wall seems twice as high as it

444
00:14:59,364 --> 00:15:02,103
did from the roof of the truck on day 70.

445
00:15:02,701 --> 00:15:05,113
Halfway up, your head rises above the

446
00:15:05,113 --> 00:15:08,094
top of the wall, and you see the yard.

447
00:15:08,094 --> 00:15:09,312
Thousands of them.

448
00:15:09,312 --> 00:15:11,044
Shoulder to shoulder,

449
00:15:11,044 --> 00:15:13,795
from the gate all the way to the canopy.

450
00:15:13,795 --> 00:15:15,881
Packed around the coach,

451
00:15:15,881 --> 00:15:18,532
where eleven faces press against the glass.

452
00:15:18,922 --> 00:15:20,416
The rain is drawing them in

453
00:15:20,416 --> 00:15:23,241
under the canopy, slowly, like a tide.

454
00:15:23,241 --> 00:15:25,400
But the ones nearest the wall

455
00:15:25,400 --> 00:15:28,381
notice something moving above them.

456
00:15:28,381 --> 00:15:29,676
One face tilts up.

457
00:15:29,676 --> 00:15:30,567
Then ten.

458
00:15:31,043 --> 00:15:31,838
Then a hundred.

459
00:15:31,838 --> 00:15:33,270
They can't climb.

460
00:15:33,270 --> 00:15:35,067
They can only watch.

461
00:15:35,067 --> 00:15:37,312
You have never been watched by

462
00:15:37,312 --> 00:15:39,276
so many people in your life,

463
00:15:39,276 --> 00:15:41,750
and that includes the day you shouted.

464
00:15:42,109 --> 00:15:43,254
Nora and Wes pull.

465
00:15:43,254 --> 00:15:45,123
You come over the rail and lie

466
00:15:45,123 --> 00:15:46,079
on the gantry,

467
00:15:46,079 --> 00:15:49,555
soaking wet, like something the river washed up.

468
00:15:50,057 --> 00:15:51,410
Nobody says a word.

469
00:15:51,410 --> 00:15:54,583
Nothing up here is allowed to be loud.

470
00:15:54,583 --> 00:15:56,659
Nora kneels down beside you.

471
00:15:56,659 --> 00:15:59,768
She knocks on your shoulder three times.

472
00:15:59,768 --> 00:16:01,819
Then a fourth time, softer.

473
00:16:02,401 --> 00:16:04,813
You start laughing without making a sound,

474
00:16:04,813 --> 00:16:08,407
and for a long time, you can't stop.

475
00:16:08,407 --> 00:16:11,616
On day 116, you wake up in the bunker,

476
00:16:11,616 --> 00:16:13,638
and there is a ceiling again.

477
00:16:13,638 --> 00:16:15,630
It has thousands of feet on it.

478
00:16:15,630 --> 00:16:16,376
On day one,

479
00:16:16,376 --> 00:16:19,912
it had hundreds, and you lay awake all night.

480
00:16:19,912 --> 00:16:22,756
Now you sleep for eleven hours straight.

481
00:16:22,756 --> 00:16:25,205
Wes is back on the stairs with his hat

482
00:16:25,205 --> 00:16:27,925
over his eyes, snoring so softly that

483
00:16:27,925 --> 00:16:30,906
Nora decides to allow it all over again.

484
00:16:30,906 --> 00:16:34,031
You empty the orange bag on the bench.

485
00:16:34,031 --> 00:16:34,802
Six apples.

486
00:16:34,802 --> 00:16:37,208
Nora holds one up to the lantern for

487
00:16:37,208 --> 00:16:39,606
a long time, as if it might be a trick.

488
00:16:40,059 --> 00:16:42,008
Then the three of you discover that

489
00:16:42,008 --> 00:16:44,787
apples are the loudest food in the world.

490
00:16:44,787 --> 00:16:47,671
Every bite cracks like a branch.

491
00:16:47,671 --> 00:16:49,098
You end up sucking on

492
00:16:49,098 --> 00:16:51,044
each piece until it gives up.

493
00:16:51,044 --> 00:16:53,259
Wes studies the chart on the wall.

494
00:16:53,259 --> 00:16:56,111
The people met column still has one mark.

495
00:16:56,111 --> 00:16:57,676
You take the pencil.

496
00:16:57,676 --> 00:17:00,722
You met a dozen people on the hill,

497
00:17:00,722 --> 00:17:02,452
but you add only one mark.

498
00:17:02,452 --> 00:17:03,853
The other eleven never

499
00:17:03,853 --> 00:17:05,795
turned a truck around for you.

500
00:17:05,795 --> 00:17:07,164
"What's she like?"

501
00:17:07,164 --> 00:17:08,040
Wes asks.

502
00:17:08,040 --> 00:17:09,791
You think about it.

503
00:17:09,791 --> 00:17:10,879
"Goggles."

504
00:17:11,332 --> 00:17:12,143
He nods,

505
00:17:12,143 --> 00:17:15,026
as if that explains everything.

506
00:17:15,026 --> 00:17:16,700
On day 117,

507
00:17:16,700 --> 00:17:20,199
the hill's radio sits on the rack next to the depot's.

508
00:17:20,199 --> 00:17:21,135
Two radios.

509
00:17:21,572 --> 00:17:22,625
For the first time,

510
00:17:22,625 --> 00:17:24,626
the bunker can listen to the hill and

511
00:17:24,626 --> 00:17:26,707
talk to it at the same time.

512
00:17:26,707 --> 00:17:29,557
Wes says it is the most luxurious

513
00:17:29,557 --> 00:17:31,759
thing that has ever happened to him.

514
00:17:31,759 --> 00:17:33,606
And outside, in the rain,

515
00:17:33,606 --> 00:17:37,440
the sign over the street says THREE ALIVE,

516
00:17:37,440 --> 00:17:39,607
and it is true again.

517
00:17:39,607 --> 00:17:41,287
On day 120,

518
00:17:41,287 --> 00:17:45,589
the rain finally stops, and at dawn you climb to

519
00:17:45,589 --> 00:17:47,941
the gantry to look at the town.

520
00:17:47,941 --> 00:17:48,676
The hill.

521
00:17:48,676 --> 00:17:49,784
The tanks.

522
00:17:50,206 --> 00:17:51,944
The split in the road.

523
00:17:51,944 --> 00:17:53,229
And the flyover.

524
00:17:53,229 --> 00:17:54,436
It's empty.

525
00:17:54,922 --> 00:17:56,992
For a month, the tail of the river

526
00:17:56,992 --> 00:18:00,132
stood on top of that bridge, facing nowhere.

527
00:18:00,132 --> 00:18:01,881
Now the concrete is bare

528
00:18:01,881 --> 00:18:04,140
from one railing to the other.

529
00:18:04,140 --> 00:18:06,578
A moment later, you find them.

530
00:18:07,031 --> 00:18:08,239
A long dark line,

531
00:18:08,239 --> 00:18:12,121
moving slowly along the road beyond the railway.

532
00:18:12,121 --> 00:18:12,716
Uphill.

533
00:18:12,716 --> 00:18:15,197
Rain makes them look for a roof,

534
00:18:15,197 --> 00:18:17,779
and the flyover doesn't have one.

535
00:18:17,779 --> 00:18:20,330
For five days, they have been drifting.

536
00:18:20,330 --> 00:18:22,766
And somewhere in that drift,

537
00:18:22,766 --> 00:18:25,059
every morning, they heard a horn.

538
00:18:25,059 --> 00:18:27,205
One short blast at every corner.

539
00:18:27,205 --> 00:18:29,244
Wes climbs up behind you with the

540
00:18:29,244 --> 00:18:31,627
hill's radio held against his chest.

541
00:18:31,627 --> 00:18:33,523
It is already crackling.

542
00:18:34,120 --> 00:18:35,852
"Depot," says the woman.

543
00:18:35,852 --> 00:18:39,267
For the first time, her voice is not calm.

544
00:18:39,267 --> 00:18:41,575
"They're on the hill road.

545
00:18:41,575 --> 00:18:42,482
Hundreds.

546
00:18:42,482 --> 00:18:44,115
More behind them."

547
00:18:44,115 --> 00:18:44,932
A pause.

548
00:18:44,932 --> 00:18:47,397
"I need your speaker."

549
00:18:47,397 --> 00:18:49,286
A hundred and twenty days.

550
00:18:49,286 --> 00:18:50,903
The same orange bandage.

551
00:18:50,903 --> 00:18:53,534
Nora is holding the flashlight.

552
00:18:53,534 --> 00:18:55,936
You are holding the orange bag.

553
00:18:55,936 --> 00:18:57,118
And this time,

554
00:18:57,118 --> 00:18:59,915
you are on the same side of the gate.

555
00:19:00,354 --> 00:19:03,578
Tomorrow is day 121.

556
00:19:04,130 --> 00:19:06,083
The tail you led away is climbing

557
00:19:06,083 --> 00:19:08,984
toward a dozen people and their clean water.

